        {
          "path": "_m/u/4/u/e/hopperstad-stave-church-wp/hopperstad-stave-church-01-1.jpg",
          "filename": "Hopperstad stave church 01.jpg",
          "caption": "It is baffling to think that Scandinavian stave churches, such as this one from 1140, fell out of favour in the peoples' taste and were seen as obsolete and ripe for demolition. Some were dismantled and sold around. It is not known precisely how many stave churches were built in Norway, but estimates vary between 1,000 and 2,000; of these, only less than 30 remain. This one was saved thanks to the efforts of architect Peter Andreas Blix (1831-1901), who invested considerable time and money of his own to its restoration (1884-1891).",
          "alt": "It is baffling to think that Scandinavian stave churches, such as this one from 1140, fell out of favour in the peoples' taste and were seen as obsolete and ripe for demolition. Some were dismantled and sold around. It is not known precisely how many stave churches were built in Norway, but estimates vary between 1,000 and 2,000; of these, only less than 30 remain. This one was saved thanks to the efforts of architect Peter Andreas Blix (1831-1901), who invested considerable time and money of his own to its restoration (1884-1891).",
          "width": 1408,
          "height": 1056,
          "mimeType": "image/jpeg",
          "license": "CC BY 2.0",
          "artist": "Bosc d'Anjou from New York, NY, USA",
          "sourceUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hopperstad_stave_church_01.jpg",
          "sourceType": "commons-category",
          "sourceArticleId": "hopperstad-stave-church-wp",
